concepts.body:
Every default, from font size to the color cycle, lives in `matplotlib.rcParams`, a dict-like object with keys such as `"font.size"`, `"axes.spines.top"`, `"lines.linewidth"`, `"figure.dpi"` and `"axes.prop_cycle"`. Defaults are read when an artist is created, so changes affect figures made afterwards.

Ways to change them, from most to least local:

- keyword arguments on one call (`linewidth=2`);
- `with mpl.rc_context({"font.size": 9}):` for a block of code;
- `mpl.rcParams.update({...})` or `plt.style.use(...)` for the rest of the session;
- a **style file** `house.mplstyle` with lines like `axes.grid: True`, applied with `plt.style.use("path/to/house.mplstyle")`;
- a `matplotlibrc` file in the working directory or config dir (`mpl.get_configdir()`), loaded at import.

Styles compose: `plt.style.use(["seaborn-v0_8-whitegrid", "./house.mplstyle"])` applies them in order, later ones overriding. A style file only needs the keys it changes. Distributing it inside a Python package lets you call `plt.style.use("mypkg.house")` by dotted name.

`mpl.rcdefaults()` restores the library defaults, useful in tests. The color cycle is set with `cycler`: `mpl.rcParams["axes.prop_cycle"] = cycler(color=[...])`.

Style files deliberately ignore settings unrelated to style, such as the backend, so a style cannot change how figures are displayed.
